implementationPlan.note:
RED first, confirmed by a fresh unnarrowed run: "Cannot find module './scope-git-classify.js' imported from .../scope-git-classify.test.ts", Test Files 1 failed | 45 passed (46), exit 1. The classifier is not yet an importable production surface, which is exactly the RED the step required.

Every branch of retagRefFailure is covered with code and layer as SEPARATE expectations (never one combined assertion — that is precisely how QA's killing mutation went unnoticed):
(a) already RUNNER_SCOPE_OBSERVATION_OVERFLOW stays OVERFLOW @ GIT_OBSERVER;
(b) RUNNER_SCOPE_OBSERVATION_FAILED with cause {code:"ENOBUFS"} PROMOTED to OVERFLOW @ GIT_OBSERVER — the win32 shape the production comment at scope-git.ts:197-201 exists for;
(c) unrelated code (RUNNER_SCOPE_STATUS_MALFORMED) passes through unchanged @ GIT_OBSERVER;
(d) foreign throws become RUNNER_SCOPE_OBSERVATION_FAILED @ GIT_OBSERVER, as a 5-entry it.each table;
(e) NEGATIVE CONTROL: cause {code:"ENOENT"} must NOT be promoted — without it the ENOBUFS comparison could be weakened to "has a cause at all" and every other case stays green.

Two extra guards: the foreign-throw table asserts a non-zero AND exact case count (rail: a sweep that silently produces zero cases passes); and one entry is `{code:"ENOBUFS"}` as a bare object, proving the promotion requires a ScopeObserverError rather than any object carrying that errno. Message pass-through pinned too.

Fixture note: the test builds the cause with Object.assign, mirroring runGit's private `withCause` at scope-git.ts:102-104, which is a bare assignment. That is fixture construction, not reimplementation of the property under test — the property under test is the classification, and it is asserted against the production classifier itself.
